{"user": "Why did grabbing the corners like a1 and h1 end up being such a big deal? I don't really get why they're so strong compared to other squares.", "user_ts": "2026-08-11T23:18:27.558608+00:00", "assistant": "Corners are huge because **they can never be flipped back**.\n\nA disc gets flipped only when it\u2019s trapped between two opponent discs in a line. But a corner has no square \u201cbehind\u201d it, so once Black owns **a1** or **h1**, White can\u2019t bracket it from the outside.\n\nWhy that matters:\n\n- **Permanent disc:** the corner itself is guaranteed forever.\n- **Stable edges:** discs connected to that corner along the edge often become safe too. Example: if Black has **h1**, then black discs along the top/right edge can become very hard or impossible to flip.\n- **Endgame counting:** in the last moves, permanent discs matter more than \u201cI flip 5 right now.\u201d\n- **Corner-adjacent squares are risky:** moves like b1, a2, b2 near an empty a1 can accidentally let White take the corner.\n\nIn this final board, Black owns **a1, h1, and h8**, and that helped lock down lots of stable edge discs. Final count looks like **Black 41, White 23**.", "assistant_started_ts": "2026-08-11T23:18:27.558608+00:00", "assistant_ts": "2026-08-11T23:18:32.558608+00:00", "annotation_user": {"final": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "consensus": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "votes": {"Knowledge Deficit Question": 3}, "confidence": {"Knowledge Deficit Question": 1.0}, "per_model": {"gpt": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "sonnet": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"], "llama": ["Knowledge Deficit Question"]}, "n_valid": 3, "min_votes": 2, "no_majority": false, "needs_review": false, "arbiter_used": false}, "annotation_assistant_fine": {"final": ["Board Report", "Local Justification", "General Principle"], "consensus": ["Board Report", "Local Justification", "General Principle"], "votes": {"Board Report": 3, "General Principle": 3, "Local Justification": 2}, "confidence":